      "writtenOn": "2025-01-09T17:40:04Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I don\u0027t think I made how GLES was overhauled quite as clear as I should as I should have, so I\u0027ll put it here as a separate comment:\n\nWith the current setup, OpenGL contexts are split into two parts, `GLESContext` objects and  `GLES` objects (maybe better called a GLESInstances?). The `GLESContext` is an inactive context that don\u0027t expose any actual GL functions. `GLES` objects are the objects that actually allow access to the GL functions, and (normally) come from `GLESContext::make_active`, which activates the context (if it is not already actually active, which is checked first).\n\nThe main insight is that `GLES` requires a exclusive borrow over it\u0027s `GLESContext` and the global `Window` - so it\u0027s not possible to destroy the context while it\u0027s being used, nor activate another context, nor yield the guest thread. The nice part is that this is all caught at compile time, not runtime, like the older system. \n\nTo be fair, this is how `sync_context` used to work (and I didn\u0027t realize this, which is why the changes exist at all), but it wasn\u0027t how window-internal context management used to work. With this system, there\u0027s some ugly behind the scenes work to make the window-internal context management work, but consumers outside of Window get a (actually) safe interface, which was not true before.\n\nIf it\u0027s _really_ unacceptable I can change it back but it almost certainly will cause us to have issues down the road with host context management, so I wouldn\u0027t be happy with it.\n\nI\u0027ll also probably change the OpenAL API to something similar if there isn\u0027t any big issues, so that it can also be checked at compile time.",
      "revId": "cadb94c5df2d67d0550c67c52f3df14b5ade206b",
      "serverId": "d5d70762-12d0-45a1-890d-524b12d3f735"
    },
